{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 4, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "**A Whirlwind in Japan: Cherry Blossoms and Neon Dreams**\n\nTokyo in spring is a riot of pink. Cherry blossoms (*sakura*) carpet the streets of Ueno Park, their delicate petals dancing in the breeze. I spent mornings among locals and tourists, sipping matcha lattes at tiny cafés, the scent of freshly baked *melon pan* drifting from street vendors. By evening, the city transformed—skyscrapers pulsed with neon, Shinjuku’s Golden Gai glow like a fairy-tale alleyway.\n\nA bullet train whisked me to Kyoto in three hours. The temples here were serene, their wooden gates weathered by centuries. I wandered through Arashiyama’s bamboo grove, sunlight filtering through towering stalks, then knelt in Fushimi Inari to pray among thousands of red torii gates. A stop in Nara introduced me to deer with buoyant hops and a giant bronze Buddha that dwarfed me.\n\nOsaka’s Dotonbori district was chaos in the best way—giant pufferfish signs, sizzling *takoyaki* stands, and the hum of a million voices. I lost myself in this culinary playground, cradling a bowl of * ramen* so rich it could have been soup for gods.\n\nMy trip ended in Hiroshima, quiet in comparison. The Peace Memorial Park offered a moment of reflection—lanterns floating on the river, a reminder of resilience. Japan’s blend of tradition and futurism left me breathless, its contradictions beautiful.\n\nWould I go back? Already plotting the next escape.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.07108993083238602}, {"token": "A", "logprob": -0.32481810450553894}, {"token": " Wh", "logprob": -0.0516633577644825}, {"token": "irl", "logprob": -0.014092573896050453}, {"token": "wind", "logprob": -6.282132380874828e-05}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.4394985437393188}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.3116919994354248}, {"token": ":", "logprob": -0.0872226133942604}, {"token": " Cherry", "logprob": -